\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Limitations.}
\emph{Simulated outcomes.} Six mechanisms of our choosing, four smooth in exposure, with Gaussian noise. \emph{Graph range.} The $d$ exponent rests on three degrees; thinning real graphs also changes their clustering. \emph{Estimators.} Causal message passing and graph neural networks were not run (Section~\ref{sec:design}); the adaptive H\'ajek estimator is simplified.

\paragraph{Implications.}
Regression adjustment pays when spillover is large and the response smooth; report the leverage $L$ and the estimated spillover share. Agreement with richer specifications is not evidence of small bias, and the default interval cannot be trusted when the response may be curved or thresholded; widening it by a bound on the bias covers only by width.
----
Our reading is that the error has two parts with different drivers: the \emph{variance} is the leverage of the corner contrast in the regression, which grows with degree; the \emph{bias} is the response's curvature times the raw distance from the observed exposures to the corner, which does not grow with degree for a smooth response and which no regression can estimate from data that never visit the corner.

